WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.516
We provide higher-fidelity electron-phonon
interaction data for materials where

2
00:00:05.546 --> 00:00:10.750
conventional P B E calculations fall short,
especially compounds with d electrons.

3
00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:15.796
These are materials where the electronic
structure and atomic vibrations can be

4
00:00:15.826 --> 00:00:20.274
difficult to predict. Our SCAN and r-squared
SCAN workflow is built to

5
00:00:20.304 --> 00:00:22.166
make that data more reliable.

6
00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:27.278
Cobalt oxide is a clear example. P B E
predicts a metallic

7
00:00:27.308 --> 00:00:32.621
state and spurious lattice instabilities.
Our r-squared SCAN calculations recover an

8
00:00:32.651 --> 00:00:37.901
insulating state and remove those
instabilities, with phonons closer to
experiment.

9
00:00:37.931 --> 00:00:43.118
Together with the nickel oxide benchmarks,
this shows why advanced functionals can

10
00:00:43.148 --> 00:00:48.146
provide substantially more faithful coupling
data for these d-electron systems.

11
00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:54.487
The aim is to bring accuracy, efficiency,
and transferability together. We use

12
00:00:54.517 --> 00:00:59.654
the same functional framework across
different material classes, without fitting
a

13
00:00:59.684 --> 00:01:03.637
Hubbard U for each material. That gives us a
practical route to

14
00:01:03.667 --> 00:01:08.866
higher-fidelity datasets for
superconductors, semiconductors, and energy
materials.

15
00:01:08.896 --> 00:01:12.538
Researchers can trace each result back to
its calculation.

16
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.139
Let us use magnesium diboride as a concrete
example. We start with

17
00:01:18.169 --> 00:01:23.428
the crystal structure and the VASP
calculation settings. Our published workflow
uses

18
00:01:23.458 --> 00:01:28.468
r-squared SCAN, finite displacements, and
Wannier interpolation to calculate the

19
00:01:28.498 --> 00:01:33.881
electronic bands, phonons, and electron-
phonon coupling. The screen shows the
settings

20
00:01:33.911 --> 00:01:35.810
reported for this calculation.

21
00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:42.292
The result is a material-specific coupling
spectrum. For magnesium diboride, our

22
00:01:42.322 --> 00:01:47.488
published r-squared SCAN calculation gives a
coupling strength of zero point seven

23
00:01:47.518 --> 00:01:51.120
three, compared with zero point six seven
